{{- $validOs := list "linux-arch" "linux-cachyos" -}}
{{ if has .osid $validOs -}}
#!/bin/bash
set -euo pipefail

echo "Installing GNOME Keyring and GCR SSH agent..."
paru -S --needed gnome-keyring libsecret gcr-4 seahorse

echo "Enabling GNOME Keyring user units..."
systemctl --user enable --now gnome-keyring-daemon.service gnome-keyring-daemon.socket

echo "Enabling GCR SSH agent..."
systemctl --user enable --now gcr-ssh-agent.socket

cat <<'EOF'

GNOME Keyring and the GCR SSH agent are enabled. Complete these steps:

1. Configure PAM for your login manager. For greetd, add these lines to
   /etc/pam.d/greetd. For TTY logins, add them to /etc/pam.d/login instead:

   auth       optional     pam_gnome_keyring.so
   session    optional     pam_gnome_keyring.so auto_start

   Do not add these blindly if your login manager already configures them.

2. Log out of your graphical session and log back in so PAM can unlock the
   keyring and the user environment is refreshed.

3. Open Seahorse and create or verify a keyring named Login. Set its password
   to your Linux login password so PAM can unlock it automatically.

4. Add this to ~/.ssh/config if you want SSH to load keys on first use:

   Host *
       AddKeysToAgent yes

5. To store an SSH key passphrase in GNOME Keyring, run:

   /usr/lib/seahorse/ssh-askpass ~/.ssh/id_ed25519

6. Verify the setup after logging back in:

   echo "$SSH_AUTH_SOCK"
   ssh-add -l

   The SSH agent socket should be $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/gcr/ssh. "The agent has
   no identities" is expected until a key has been added.
EOF
{{ end -}}
